Titles do not make someone more human than you, and rank does not create intrinsic superiority.

But there is a useful distinction between status and jurisdiction. A judge, commanding officer, employer, police officer, regulator, or even a parent can have bounded authority within a particular system without being “above” you as a person. You can reject humiliation, blind obedience, or the idea that their title makes them inherently superior while still recognizing what power the role actually carries.

The strongest position is not “nobody can tell me anything.” It is: “Show me the source, scope, and limit of your authority. I will decide what I accept, what I contest, and what consequences I am willing to bear.”

That keeps your agency intact without confusing dignity with immunity from consequences. It also makes you much harder to manipulate, because you are evaluating the structure instead of reacting to the person wearing the badge, rank, title, or office.